.TH std::inplace_vector::reserve 3 "2026.11.10" "http://cppreference.com" "C++ Standard Libary"
.SH NAME
std::inplace_vector::reserve \- std::inplace_vector::reserve

.SH Synopsis
static constexpr void reserve( size_type new_cap );  (since C++26)

Does nothing, except that may throw std::bad_alloc. The request to increase the capacity
(i.e., the internal storage size) is ignored because std::inplace_vector<T, N> is a
fixed-capacity container.

.SH Parameters

new_cap - new capacity of the inplace_vector, in number of elements

.SH Return value

\fI(none)\fP

.SH Complexity

Constant.

.SH Exceptions

std::bad_alloc if new_cap > capacity() is true.

.SH Notes

This function exists for compatibility with vector-like interfaces.

.SH Example


// Run this code

#include <cassert>
#include <inplace_vector>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    std::inplace_vector<int, 4> v{1, 2, 3};
    assert(v.capacity() == 4 && v.size() == 3);

    v.reserve(2); // does nothing
    assert(v.capacity() == 4 && v.size() == 3);

    try
    {
        v.reserve(13); // throws, because requested capacity > N; v is left unchanged
    }
    catch(const std::bad_alloc& ex)
    {
        std::cout << ex.what() << '\\n';
    }
    assert(v.capacity() == 4 && v.size() == 3);
}

.SH Possible output:

std::bad_alloc

